An acknowledgement while the pace forbids data, which ngtcp2 already writes and this client lost.

WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES. quic-go, asked to send while pacing-limited, parks the deadline and still
puts one packet on the wire — an acknowledgement on its own:

    case ackhandler.SendPacingLimited:
        ...
        c.pacingDeadline = deadline
        // Allow sending of an ACK if we're pacing limit.
        ...
        return c.maybeSendAckOnlyPacket(now)
                             (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:2496-2505)

WHAT NGTCP2 ALREADY DOES, and it is the same thing. In NGTCP2_CS_POST_HANDSHAKE, when its own
pacing refuses a packet, it does not return empty-handed either:

    if (!conn_pacing_pkt_tx_allowed(conn, ts)) {
      ...
      return conn_write_ack_pkt(conn, pi, dest, origlen, NGTCP2_PKT_1RTT, ts);
    }
                             (lib/ngtcp2_conn.c, ngtcp2_conn_writev_stream's switch)

HOW THIS CLIENT LOST IT. Patch 0009 moved the pacing decision OUT of ngtcp2 and into the send loop,
because the reference paces with hysteria's token bucket and ngtcp2's own pacer is a different
mechanism — `ngtcp2_conn_update_pkt_tx_time` is never called here, so `conn_pacing_pkt_tx_allowed`
is always true and that branch is unreachable. The loop asks the bucket itself and returns when the
answer is no, which is correct about DATA and wrong about acknowledgements: it never asks ngtcp2
whether one is due.

WHAT IT COST, measured through the impairing relay on 2026-09-20, same transfer both clients:
datagrams under 300 bytes were 15.6 % of what the reference sent (7 507) against 4.8 % of ours
(1 868). The 5 639 missing small packets are the same order as the 3 451 times the loop stopped on
the pace in that run (tx_stop_paced, src/quic/conn.h). They are also the whole of the back-to-back
density gap that three separate changes to the FEED never moved — read `commonest runs` on that
stand: the reference's runs of two and three datagrams are an ACK beside full packets, not a short
burst of data.

WHAT THIS PATCH ADDS: nothing but reach. conn_write_ack_pkt is already written, already handles the
1RTT space with the right ack delay and exponent, and already returns zero when no acknowledgement
is due. It is static. This exposes it for the one caller that needs it, with the two conditions its
own assert and its callers rely on checked here rather than assumed: the connection is past the
handshake with 1RTT keys, and no packet is half-built (NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_PPE_PENDING).

The bucket is charged for what goes out, because the reference charges it: hysteria's BBR calls
`b.pacer.SentPacket(sentTime, bytes)` as the first statement of OnPacketSent, before it looks at
whether the packet was ack-eliciting (core/internal/congestion/bbr/bbr_sender.go:444-451), and
quic-go calls OnPacketSent for every packet it sends, ack-eliciting or not
(internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:325).

Applies on top of 0009.
--- a/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
+++ b/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
@@ -3006,6 +3006,33 @@
   return spktlen;
 }
 
+/*
+ * ngtcp2_conn_write_ack_pkt_1rtt writes a packet containing nothing but an ACK frame, for a
+ * caller that paces outside this library and therefore never reaches the branch in
+ * ngtcp2_conn_writev_stream that would have written one. See patches/ngtcp2/0010 for why that
+ * caller exists and what the reference does at the same moment.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 - not an error - when there is no acknowledgement to send, when the connection is
+ * not past the handshake, or when a packet is half-built.
+ */
+ngtcp2_ssize ngtcp2_conn_write_ack_pkt_1rtt(ngtcp2_conn *conn,
+                                            ngtcp2_pkt_info *pi, uint8_t *dest,
+                                            size_t destlen, ngtcp2_tstamp ts) {
+  if (conn->state != NGTCP2_CS_POST_HANDSHAKE) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+
+  if (conn->pktns.crypto.tx.ckm == NULL) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+
+  if (conn->flags & NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_PPE_PENDING) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+
+  return conn_write_ack_pkt(conn, pi, dest, destlen, NGTCP2_PKT_1RTT, ts);
+}
+
